From: Henrique Reis Neves <39978017+NevesHenry@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 4 Nov 2026 18:57:46 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] kernel: export sceKernelDeleteTimerEvent (Gran Turismo 7)
 (#2107)

sceKernelDeleteTimerEvent (YWQFUyXIVdU) was not registered, although
sceKernelAddTimerEvent and sceKernelDeleteHRTimerEvent are. A call to it
landed in the unresolved import stub, which returns 0 and leaves the
timer in the queue, so a periodic timer the game had deleted kept firing.

Gran Turismo 7 1.000 (PPSA01316, #1930) does this in its "PGL EventQ"
thread: each EVFILT_TIMER event (ident 1) is followed by a
sceKernelDeleteTimerEvent call. The log attached to #1930 reaches the
1024-call stub log cap after about 13 seconds, and the thread keeps
receiving about 80 timer events per second next to 60 graphics events.

Delete the EVFILT_TIMER event with the existing KernelDeleteEvent, the
same way sceKernelDeleteHRTimerEvent deletes EVFILT_HRTIMER events. An
unknown queue gives EBADF and an unknown timer gives ENOENT.

The periodic timer test now deletes its timers through the export and
checks that a deleted zero-interval timer is not delivered again.

Co-authored-by: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
(cherry picked from commit a7e14aa716844006e583516f457f452c58815a80)
---
 src/kernel/eventQueue.cpp         |  4 ++++
 src/kernel/eventQueue.h           |  1 +
 src/libs/libKernel.cpp            |  1 +
 tests/EventQueueLifetimeTests.cpp | 10 ++++++----
 4 files changed, 12 insertions(+), 4 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/kernel/eventQueue.cpp b/src/kernel/eventQueue.cpp
index fd6a72e0..4a89df9c 100644
--- a/src/kernel/eventQueue.cpp
+++ b/src/kernel/eventQueue.cpp
@@ -533,6 +533,10 @@ int KYTY_SYSV_ABI KernelAddTimerEvent(KernelEqueue eq, int id, KernelUseconds us
 	return AddTimerEvent(eq, id, static_cast<uint64_t>(usec) * 1000, true, udata);
 }
 
+int KYTY_SYSV_ABI KernelDeleteTimerEvent(KernelEqueue eq, int id) {
+	return KernelDeleteEvent(eq, static_cast<uintptr_t>(id), KERNEL_EVFILT_TIMER);
+}
+
 int KYTY_SYSV_ABI KernelAddHRTimerEvent(KernelEqueue eq, int id, const KernelTimespec* ts,
                                         void* udata) {
 	if (ts == nullptr) {
